{:db/id 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186128972 :question/id #uuid "6d363483-4ce5-5c17-8ab8-11bfcf8f9282" :question/math-academy-id "e-5888" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d6f70aed4d013bfcdd9060124ad003e3e1ed6ce70852e4f60d71cdd8d65cc4.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x = -1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\n- $x=-3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\n- $p(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x = -1$ has an even multiplicity because at $x = -1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement II is true. The roots $x = -3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the points, but the curve isn't tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186048691 :topic/math-academy-id 1654 :topic/title "Graphing Cubic Curves Containing Three Distinct Real Roots"} {:db/id 17592186048799 :topic/math-academy-id 1656 :topic/title "Graphing Cubic Curves Containing a Double Root"} {:db/id 17592186049105 :topic/math-academy-id 2084 :topic/title "Graphing Cubic Curves Containing One Distinct Real Root"} {:db/id 17592186050046 :topic/math-academy-id 2050 :topic/title "End Behavior of Polynomials"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186129698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e76d3042-f7ae-57ef-b396-eb6626b7976b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186439667} {:answer/id #uuid "2a530406-e55f-5a02-bcae-6dda607cc6d1" :answer/type {